# KNOWN GAP — a producer whose emitted C DECLARES a borrowed view but RETURNS
# an owned heap string: the caller's temp is never dropped. `todo/t0609` item 2.
#
# THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE PRODUCER'S DECLARED RETURN TYPE, and it is visible in
# the emitted C. Measured at R47 close, all four in the identical `print(...)`
# argument position:
#     print(s.trim().to_upper())  -> CLEAN   `static inline GorgetString gorget_str_to_upper`
#     print(",".join(parts))      -> CLEAN   `static inline GorgetString gorget_str_join`
#     print(x.debug())            -> LEAKS   `static inline Str      gorget_int_to_str`
#     print(f.display())          -> LEAKS   `static inline Str      gorget_float_to_str`
# `Str` is the VIEW type — a borrowed slice, nothing for the caller to drop —
# yet those two bodies `GORGET_ALLOC` and `return gorget_string_adopt(out)`,
# i.e. they hand back an OWNED allocation. The drop machinery reads the declared
# type, correctly concludes there is nothing to drop, and the temp is lost. The
# write site is lying to it (Core #2; layering rule 1 names view-vs-owned as an
# invariant a layer may not drop). Frames: `str_alloc_copy` <-
# `gorget_string_adopt` <- `gorget_int_to_str` / `gorget_float_to_str` <- `main`.
#
# ⚠ IT IS NOT "BUILTIN METHODS LEAK", AND IT IS NOT "CALL RESULT TEMPS LEAK".
# Both are refuted by cells in this file: `to_upper` and `join` are builtin
# methods in the same position and are clean, and a plain USER function
# returning a heap String there is clean too. Every earlier attempt to state
# this class more broadly than the measurement was wrong; if you widen it again,
# probe first.
#
# ⚠ THE TWO CLEAN BUILTIN CELLS ARE THE POINT OF THE NET, not decoration. A fix
# that drop-registers every builtin-method result temp — the scope the earlier
# version of this header implied — DOUBLE FREES `to_upper` and `join`, whose
# emitted helpers already free their result. Those cells are what trips.
#
# ⚠ HEAP-FORCED throughout: `x.debug()` / `f.display()` allocate (3 and 4
# bytes), `heavy` concatenates, and `s.trim().to_upper()` allocates. A
# literal-only version leaks nothing observable and is a false negative (the
# lesson `todo/t0705` was filed for).
#
# ⚠ ONLY LeakSanitizer CAN SEE THIS — stdout is correct today, so this is wired
# to `assert_gg_sanitize_clean` (`detect_leaks=1`), never to `run_gg`.
#
# Un-ignore + promote out of `known_gaps/` when the producers are declared to
# return what they actually return.

from std.collections import Vector

String heavy(String a, String b):
    return a + b

int main():
    int x = 21
    float f = 7.5
    String s = "  hi  "
    Vector[String] parts = Vector[String]()
    parts.push("a")
    parts.push("b")

    # CONTROL — a USER call's result temp at a call-arg position. Clean today.
    print(heavy("alpha", "beta"))

    # CONTROL — BUILTIN methods at the same position whose emitted producer is
    # declared `GorgetString` and frees its result. Clean today, and exactly
    # what an over-wide fix would turn into a double free.
    print(s.trim().to_upper())
    print(",".join(parts))

    # THE GAP — builtin methods whose emitted producer is declared `Str` while
    # returning an owned allocation.
    print(x.debug())
    print(f.display())

    # CONTROL — the same leaking producers, bound to a named local first. Clean
    # today, because binding drop-registers the value; also a double-free cell
    # for any fix that registers unconditionally.
    String bound = heavy("gamma", "delta")
    print(bound)
    String d = x.debug()
    print(d)
    return 0
